P(8,"One of the first things Peter did next day was to measure Wendy and John and Michael",
 "Each boy needs a hollow tree that fits him. Going up and down is easy only if the tree fits.",
 "One of the first things Peter did next day was to measure Wendy and John and Michael for hollow trees. Hook, you remember, had laughed at the boys for thinking they needed a tree each. But he did not know. Unless your tree fitted you, it was hard to go up and down, and no two of the boys were quite the same size. When you fitted, you drew in your breath at the top, and down you went at just the right speed. To go up, you drew in and let out your breath one after the other, and so wriggled up. When you have learned how, you can do these things without thinking about them, and then nothing can be more graceful.",
 ['apiece','ignorance','ascend','alternately','graceful'])
P(8,"Adventures, of course, as we shall see, were of daily occurrence",
 "Peter invents a game of pretending not to have adventures. He likes it for a while and then loses interest.",
 "Adventures happened every day, as we shall see. But at this time Peter invented, with Wendy's help, a new game that he liked very much, until he suddenly lost all interest in it. As you have been told, this always happened with his games. The game was to pretend not to have adventures. It meant doing what John and Michael had done all their lives: sitting on stools and throwing balls in the air, pushing each other, going out for walks and coming back without having killed even a grizzly. It was a great sight to see Peter doing nothing on a stool. He could not help looking serious at such times, because sitting still seemed to him such a funny thing to do. He said proudly that he had gone for a walk for the good of his health. For several suns these were the newest of all adventures to him. John and Michael had to pretend to be happy about it too, or he would have been hard on them.",
 ['occurrence','boasted','novel','severely'])
S(8,"while you have to be made to fit the tree",
 "Peter measures you for your tree as carefully as for a suit of clothes. The only difference is that clothes are made to fit you, but you have to be made to fit the tree.",
 "'the only difference being that...' gives the difference after a colon; 'are made to fit' and 'have to be made to fit' are passive.")
S(8,"As time wore on did she think much about the beloved parents",
 "As time passed, did she think much about the dear parents she had left behind?",
 "'As time wore on' is a time clause that means 'as time passed slowly'; 'she had left' is the past perfect for an earlier time.")
S(8,"he could not stodge just to feel stodgy",
 "He could really eat if it was part of a game, but he could not stuff himself with heavy food just to feel stuffed.",
 "'just to feel stodgy' (to + verb) gives the reason; 'stodge' is a verb and 'stodgy' is an adjective from the same word.")
